{"t":"msg","chat":"m2resume","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Maintain a consistent sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, even on weekends, to regulate your body's internal clock.\n2. Create a relaxing bedtime routine that helps you unwind, such as reading a book, taking a warm bath, or practicing gentle stretching, so your brain associates these activities with sleep.\n3. Make your bedroom environment conducive to rest by keeping it cool, dark, and quiet, and investing in a comfortable mattress and pillows that support your preferred sleeping position.\n4. Limit exposure to screens and blue light at least one hour before bed, since the light emitted by phones, tablets, and computers can suppress melatonin production and delay sleep onset.\n5. Avoid caffeine and other stimulants in the late afternoon and evening, as they can linger in your system for several hours and interfere with your ability to fall asleep.\n6. Get regular physical activity during the day, but try to finish vigorous workouts at least three hours before bedtime so your body has time to wind down.\n7. Be mindful of your meals and avoid heavy, spicy, or large meals close to bedtime, while also limiting excessive fluid intake to reduce nighttime awakenings.\n8. If you cannot fall asleep after about twenty minutes, get out of bed and do a quiet, low-light activity until you feel sleepy, then return to bed to strengthen the association between bed and sleep.\n9. Manage stress and racing thoughts through techniques like deep breathing, meditation, or journaling before bed, since mental arousal is one of the most common barriers to restful sleep.\n10. Reserve your bed primarily for sleep and intimacy, avoiding work, eating, or prolonged wakefulness in bed, so that your brain learns to associate it with rest rather than activity."}],"seq":138}
